I solved my problem but the solution is bad because it doesn't generalize well.

Given toy df:

rainfall <- data.frame(dates = c(as.Date("2014-03-01")+0:6,as.Date("2015-03-01")+0:6), rain = runif(14,0,4))

Suppose we want to compare the cumulative rainfall for the first week of March in each year.

This is close:

library(lubridate) # for day() and year() fns
library(ggplot2)

ggplot(rainfall) + geom_step(aes(x = day(dates), y = cumsum(rain), color = as.factor(year(dates))))

But that isn't quite it: we'd like to have both months start at the same point for easy visual comparison.

Here's an ugly solution:

library(tidyr) # for spread()
library(dplyr)

df <- rainfall %>% mutate(day = day(dates), year = year(dates)) %>% select(day, year, rain) %>% spread(year, rain)

names(df) <- c("day", "yr_2014", "yr_2015")  # an ugly step, necessary b/c spread() turned years into var names in the new df

ggplot(df) + geom_step(aes(x = day, y = cumsum(yr_2014)), color = "blue")
+ geom_step(aes(x = day, y = cumsum(yr_2015)), color = "red")

This works, but i'd like to avoid the un-tidying/ renaming/ etc of my data.

Is there a better way?

From u27405c4b711a1080 at groups.google.com  Fri Apr  1 16:33:00 2016
From: u27405c4b711a1080 at groups.google.com (Brandon Hurr)
Date: Fri, 01 Apr 2016 16:33:00 +0000
Subject: Re: overlay two step plots
Message-ID: <_L021DiYptU.qKEPz0DWCAAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <_L021DiYptU.RjJF2xjVCAAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <_L021DiYptU.RjJF2xjVCAAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/_L021DiYptU/m/qKEPz0DWCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

By adding a grouping, then cumsum'ing within that group then plotting it does the same thing without the need for tidyr:

rainfall <- data.frame(dates = c(as.Date("2014-03-01")+0:6,as.Date("2015-03-01")+0:6), rain = runif(14,0,4))

rainfall %>%
mutate(day = day(dates), year = year(dates)) %>%
select(day, year, rain) %>%

group_by(year) %>%
mutate(cumsumrain = cumsum(rain)) %>%
ggplot(data=., aes(x=day, y=cumsumrain, colour=as.factor(year))) +
geom_step()

Thanks for the reproducible example!

B

